WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:09.040
Your grandmother. Today you're a
grandmother yourself. Are you like her?

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.360
-Well...
-She was an important person.

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:18.040
I sometimes think about this.
She was a very strict, pedantic-

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.720
-and industrious woman.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:24.600
I think have inherited
some of those things.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:31.480
Pedantry can be both good and bad.
But I have that.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:36.760
-You clean a lot?
-You'll always see me with my duster...

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:42.960
I'm a pedant in many other respects
as well. In life, simply.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:49.040
And so was she. She brought me up,
so of course I copied her-

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:54.640
-and was shaped by her way of being,
towards herself and towards me too.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:57.720
-Are you strict as well?
-No, I'm a kind person.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:01.800
But sometimes I'm exacting.
It depends on the situation.

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:09.360
But your husband -
we met him backstage - Henry...

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:13.240
-Is he like you?
-No, he's the opposite.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:16.200
-I see.
-It's a good combination.

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.560
-Yes, but...
-Can you relate to this?

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:22.120
-Do you correct him sometimes?
-He said that?

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:25.840
I'm just...

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.440
Yes, I do correct him sometimes.

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:33.040
But not in a...
I do so in a friendly manner.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:36.560
-I'm a super-pedant myself.
-So, you get me.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:41.280
One-hundred-percent. Look around.
It's really nice and tidy here.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:45.520
-You're very diligent.
-It's so beautiful.

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:49.120
-It is.
-It's really important.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:54.600
You sing, you write
and you design clothes.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:57.320
Yes, and it's such fun.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:02.080
A couple of years ago, when I realized I
was quite done with singing, I thought:

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:07.280
"What would I like to replace it with?"
And I need to do something creative.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:10.400
I've always liked design.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:14.880
And since I spend so much time
out in nature, skiing-

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:18.680
-so, what are my thoughts
on today's ski outfits? They're boring.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.360
They're quite unisex.
Very practical, of course.

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:29.680
But I wanted to go back to the more
feminine ski suit from the 70's.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:33.520
-ABBA-clothes?
-Yes, we did wear such suits, yes.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:37.560
-Björn Ulvaeus had one of those...
-I wasn't thinking about ABBA.

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:41.600
I was thinking about when I went skiing.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.440
-And then...
-It was all about air resistance.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.320
Really? Well...
Yes, it's quite fast, sometimes.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:53.360
Anyways, I designed a ski suit.
Of the rather exclusive kind.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:55.640
I'm very pleased with it.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:00.680
And then I asked around if anybody'd be
interested in producing it for me.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:03.000
I didn't want to do that myself.

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:08.960
A fashion company in Sweden was
interested. They're also international.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:13.320
We'll launch the outfits
in December of this year.

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:16.840
-Your ski outfits.
-Yes.

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:26.120
You've been praised a lot
for your business skills.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:29.120
You've made good investments and...

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.600
-Both yes and no, really.
-Sure. Those things go up and down.

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:39.120
But you've been good at handling
your economy.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:42.720
It goes hand in hand with the pedantry.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:46.000
Did you use to be different?

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:50.160
I was more spendthrift.
I liked shopping and things.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:56.040
When you've never had any money
it's fun, naturally.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:59.960
But I'm as careful now, as when
I travelled to Norway as a child-

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:05.160
-and collected pennies in a bag
that I brought with me to buy candy.

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:12.240
But I couldn't use Swedish pennies in
Norway, so somebody else had to pay.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:18.520
You have fans all over the world.

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:22.520
They sent us messages
before your arrival.

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:27.440
Do you become friends 
with them over time?

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:30.200
Or do you send them a polite reply
and that's it?

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:36.640
I've become friends with some of them
and remain in contact with them.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:41.120
There is one fan in particular.
He contacted me four years ago.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:45.200
I was very moved by his story.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:52.400
He's of simple origins. He was about 60
years old when he started writing to me.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:57.680
He trained as a butcher at sixteen
and when the foot-and-mouth disease-

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.480
-hit England in 2002-

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:08.040
-he was called upon by the authorities
to help slaughter the sick cattle.

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:11.720
It was a horrible time for him-

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:16.400
-because he had to slaughter
all these animals-

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:23.840
-in a very particular way and also had
to share these families' griefs-

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:27.360
-and their loss
of their means of livelihood.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:32.560
This resulted
in his getting deeply depressed-

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:37.400
-and he eventually decided
to take his own life.

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:41.840
He was about to go through with it.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:46.440
He took his favourite ABBA-tape
and his gun-

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:50.040
-and went out to end it all.

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:57.360
Just as he's ready, ABBA:s singing: 
"The Way Old Friends Do".

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:00.560
He stops and listens to the these lyrics
and he's thinking:

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:06.160
"This is exactly how life is."
You need to be with your friends-

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:11.960
-and you need to help each other out,
the way old friends do.

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:15.360
He'd left his wife without a word,
of course.

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:18.680
He had children and grandchildren.
He had a big family.

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:22.560
So, he changed his mind,
because of this song.

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:26.000
He went back to his wife
and told her everything.

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:29.600
And then I received a letter
where he told me this whole story.

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:33.520
We continued to correspond.
I was very moved, of course.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:37.560
I replied with a long
and friendly letter.

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:42.600
And then we continued to keep in
contact. He'd write me once a month.

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:46.680
He'd tell me about his life, 
his family, his friends-

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:54.040
-his hobbies - everything.
He was a wonderful writer.

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:58.800
And then what happened was
- he got lung cancer.

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:03.120
And he passed away last February
and that was very sad.

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:06.960
-It was great having you here, Frida.
-Thank you.

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:11.640
All the way from Switzerland
- here's Frida.

